  "summary": "Aneurin “Nye” Bevan was a Welsh Labour politician who led the creation of Britain’s National Health Service and helped establish the welfare state. Born in 1897 to a mining family in Tredegar, he left school at 14, worked in coal mines and became a miners’ union activist. He led local miners during the 1926 General Strike and entered Parliament as MP for Ebbw Vale in 1929.\n\nBevan became known for forceful criticism of politicians across party lines, including Winston Churchill. During the Second World War, he opposed aspects of the government’s policies and called for broader action against fascism. In Clement Attlee’s postwar government, he became Minister of Health. Drawing on the Tredegar Medical Aid Society, he campaigned for healthcare free at the point of use. The NHS began in 1948 after the 1946 Act, bringing more than 2,500 hospitals into public ownership.\n\nBevan resigned as Minister of Labour in 1951 over proposed healthcare charges and funding for rearmament. He later held senior Labour opposition roles and became deputy leader in 1959. He died of stomach cancer in 1960, aged 62.",
